From: curl-impersonate maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Use Fil-C-compatible BoringSSL fallbacks

Fil-C rejects unknown madvise advice values instead of returning EINVAL.
BoringSSL probes madvise with -1 before enabling MADV_WIPEONFORK, so skip that
probe and report the optimization unavailable. BoringSSL then draws fresh OS
entropy on each call, its documented safe fallback when fork detection is not
available.

BoringSSL's specialized P-256 method also passes pointers through integer
words in generated Bedrock code, which discards Fil-C's pointer capabilities.
Use BoringSSL's generic Montgomery method for P-256 under Fil-C, as it already
does for curves without a compatible specialized method.

--- a/crypto/fipsmodule/ec/ec.cc.inc
+++ b/crypto/fipsmodule/ec/ec.cc.inc
@@ -99,7 +99,9 @@ DEFINE_METHOD_FUNCTION(EC_GROUP, EC_group_p256) {
   ec_group_init_static_mont(&out->order, std::size(kP256Order), kP256Order,
                             kP256OrderRR, kP256OrderN0);
 
-#if !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM) &&                              \
+#if defined(__FILC__)
+  out->meth = EC_GFp_mont_method();
+#elif !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM) &&                           \
     (defined(OPENSSL_X86_64) || defined(OPENSSL_AARCH64)) && \
     !defined(OPENSSL_SMALL)
   out->meth = EC_GFp_nistz256_method();

--- a/crypto/rand/fork_detect.cc
+++ b/crypto/rand/fork_detect.cc
@@ -57,6 +57,9 @@ using namespace bssl;
 
 static bool wipeonfork(void *addr, size_t page_size) {
 #if defined(OPENSSL_LINUX)
+#if defined(__FILC__)
+  return false;
+#else
   // Linux flavor, >=4.14.
   // Some versions of qemu (up to at least 5.0.0-rc4, see linux-user/syscall.c)
   // ignore `madvise` calls and just return zero (i.e. success). But we need to
@@ -66,6 +69,7 @@ static bool wipeonfork(void *addr, size_t page_size) {
   // unknown `advice` values.
   return madvise(addr, page_size, -1) != 0 &&
          madvise(addr, page_size, MADV_WIPEONFORK) == 0;
+#endif
 #elif defined(MAP_INHERIT_ZERO)
   // OpenBSD flavor, >=5.6.
   return minherit(addr, page_size, MAP_INHERIT_ZERO) == 0;
